4.text:
The symmetry of the centered Greek cross is correctly noted by the majority, but it does not force all non-constant rows inside the canton to share an identical support vector. Rows intersecting only the vertical arm produce a narrow central red interruption (b-a-b with a narrow a-block whose width equals the vertical-arm thickness). Rows intersecting the horizontal arm instead produce a strictly wider central red interruption (same b-a-b form, but with a wider a-block whose width equals the full horizontal-arm length). These two distinct nonzero supports are linearly independent after subtraction of the constant-a background (neither is a scalar multiple of the other, and the arm-overlap geometry prevents one from being recovered from the other plus the exterior field).  

The four white rectangles therefore induce exactly two distinct row supports (and two column supports) inside the canton. This yields a rank-2 update \(W\) to the rank-1 all-ones matrix, so \(\operatorname{rank}(M)=3\) for generic \(a\neq b\). The same bound holds column-wise and is attained on any faithful discretization. All rank-2 claims rest on erroneously treating the two white-row patterns as identical once the vertical arm is imposed.

